 {"step":19,"text":"== stage ==\nStep 10 of 11\nYour credit cards\nYou told us you pay for extras that a credit card sometimes already covers, so we want to know which cards you carry.\nWhich of these credit cards do you carry?\nSome cards come with perks that overlap with the extras you picked, like rental car coverage or trip protection. We only use this to point you to the right fine print. Tap all that apply.\n[Chase Sapphire Preferred] [Chase Sapphire Reserve] [Chase Freedom Unlimited] [Chase Freedom Flex] [American Express Platinum] [American Express Gold] [American Express Blue Cash Preferred] [American Express Blue Cash Everyday] [Citi Double Cash] [Wells Fargo Active Cash] [Capital One Venture X] [Capital One Venture] [Another card, or no credit card]\nAnswer every question to continue. Where it is offered, Not sure is a fine answer.\n[Back] [Continue (disabled)]\n== known ==\nAuto insurance\nHomeowners insurance\nRenters insurance\nUmbrella insurance\nHealth insurance\nMotorcycle insurance\nBoat insurance\nRental car insurance\nA towing or roadside plan\nTravel insurance for a trip\nPhone insurance\nExtended warranties on electronics or appliances\nAccidental death coverage\n== progress ==\n82% Step 10 of 11"},
 {"step":20,"text":"== stage ==\nStep 10 of 11\nYour credit cards\nYou told us you pay for extras that a credit card sometimes already covers, so we want to know which cards you carry.\nWhich of these credit cards do you carry?\nSome cards come with perks that overlap with the extras you picked, like rental car coverage or trip protection. We only use this to point you to the right fine print. Tap all that apply.\n[*Chase Sapphire Preferred] [Chase Sapphire Reserve] [Chase Freedom Unlimited] [Chase Freedom Flex] [American Express Platinum] [American Express Gold] [American Express Blue Cash Preferred] [American Express Blue Cash Everyday] [Citi Double Cash] [*Wells Fargo Active Cash] [Capital One Venture X] [Capital One Venture] [Another card, or no credit card]\nWhen you rent a car, book a trip, or buy electronics, do you usually pay with one of those cards?\nA card's perks usually only apply to what you charge to that card.\n[*Yes] [No] [Not sure]\n[Back] [Continue]\n== known ==\nAuto insurance\nHomeowners insurance\nRenters insurance\nUmbrella insurance\nHealth insurance\nMotorcycle insurance\nBoat insurance\nRental car insurance\nA towing or roadside plan\nTravel insurance for a trip\nPhone insurance\nExtended warranties on electronics or appliances\nAccidental death coverage\n== progress ==\n82% Step 10 of 11"},
 {"step":21,"text":"== stage ==\nStep 11 of 11, then your list\nA little more about your extras\nYou told us about a few extras, so here are a couple of follow-ups to see whether you are covered twice.\nDo you pay your phone bill with a credit card?\nWe ask so we can point you to the right fine print on your card.\n[Yes] [No] [Not sure]\nWhich of these already give you roadside help or towing?\nIf more than one of these gives you the same help, you may be paying twice. Tap all that apply.\n[A roadside add-on on my car insurance] [An auto club membership, like AAA] [A credit card that includes roadside help] [Roadside help that came with my car] [Something else] [Not sure]\nIs your towing or roadside plan on your motorcycle or boat policy?\nTowing or roadside help can be part of a motorcycle or boat policy, and your car's roadside help usually does not reach those vehicles. We ask so we can tell whether you are covered twice or not at all.\n[Yes] [No] [Not sure]\nBesides accidental death coverage, do you have regular life insurance, like term life?\nRegular life insurance pays your family if you die while the policy is active. Accidental death coverage pays only when an accident causes the death.\n[Yes, a policy I bought myself] [Yes, but only through my job] [No] [Not sure]\nAnswer every question to continue. Where it is offered, Not sure is a fine answer.\n[Back] [Continue (disabled)]\n== known ==\nAuto insurance\nHomeowners insurance\nRenters insurance\nUmbrella insurance\nHealth insurance\nMotorcycle insurance\nBoat insurance\nRental car insurance\nA towing or roadside plan\nTravel insurance for a trip\nPhone insurance\nExtended warranties on electronics or appliances\nAccidental death coverage\n== progress ==\n91% Step 11 of 11, then your list"},
 {"step":22,"text":"== stage ==\nStep 11 of 11, then your list\nA little more about your extras\nYou told us about a few extras, so here are a couple of follow-ups to see whether you are covered twice.\nDo you pay your phone bill with a credit card?\nWe ask so we can point you to the right fine print on your card.\n[Yes] [*No] [Not sure]\nWhich of these already give you roadside help or towing?\nIf more than one of these gives you the same help, you may be paying twice. Tap all that apply.\n[A roadside add-on on my car insurance] [An auto club membership, like AAA] [*A credit card that includes roadside help] [*Roadside help that came with my car] [Something else] [Not sure]\nIs your towing or roadside plan on your motorcycle or boat policy?\nTowing or roadside help can be part of a motorcycle or boat policy, and your car's roadside help usually does not reach those vehicles. We ask so we can tell whether you are covered twice or not at all.\n[*Yes] [No] [Not sure]\nBesides accidental death coverage, do you have regular life insurance, like term life?\nRegular life insurance pays your family if you die while the policy is active. Accidental death coverage pays only when an accident causes the death.\n[Yes, a policy I bought myself] [Yes, but only through my job] [*No] [Not sure]\n[Back] [Continue]\n== known ==\nAuto insurance\nHomeowners insurance\nRenters insurance\nUmbrella insurance\nHealth insurance\nMotorcycle insurance\nBoat insurance\nRental car insurance\nA towing or roadside plan\nTravel insurance for a trip\nPhone insurance\nExtended warranties on electronics or appliances\nAccidental death coverage\n== progress ==\n91% Step 11 of 11, then your list"},
 {"step":23,"text":"== stage ==\nYour list\nYour insurer action list\n22 items to take to your insurer: 4 Raise, 2 Drop, 16 Question.\nTake this list to your insurer or agent. Each item says why it matters, what to ask and what to say.\nThis list is general information, not insurance advice, and this tool is not a licensed agent or broker. It is based only on the answers you gave, so it can miss things. Check your own policy documents, and talk to your insurer or agent before you change any coverage.\n[Copy my list] [Print my list]\nYour umbrella and what sits under it\nAn umbrella pays only after the liability limit on the policy underneath has been used up, so each limit below is where the umbrella can start to pay for that policy. Your umbrella insurer sets the minimum it wants underneath.\nUmbrella\nSits on top of the policies below\nUmbrella limit: $5 million\nAuto liability\nBelow what your umbrella asks for. Ask your insurer to raise it.\nYour limit: 100/300\nYour umbrella asks for: 250/500\nHome liability\nMeets what your umbrella asks for\nYour limit: $500,000\nYour umbrella asks for: $500,000\nMotorcycle liability\nBelow what your umbrella asks for. Ask your insurer to raise it.\nYour limit: 250/500\nYour umbrella asks for: 500/500\nBoat liability\nBelow what your umbrella asks for. Ask your insurer to raise it.\nYour limit: Lower than 100/300\nYour umbrella asks for: 100/300\nRaise (4)\nLimits that look too low, or gaps in your coverage to close.\nRaise\nRaise your auto liability limit to what your umbrella asks for\nWhy it matters\nAuto liability limits are usually written as three numbers, like 250/500/100: the most for one person you hurt, the most for everyone you hurt in one accident, and the most for property damage, in thousands of dollars. This tool asks only about the first two. An umbrella is extra liability coverage that starts paying only after those limits are used up, and umbrella insurers set a minimum for what has to be underneath. You told us yours asks for 250/500 and your auto policy has 100/300. As a rough yardstick, the Insurance Information Institute says most insurers want about $250,000 of auto liability under a $1 million umbrella, but it does not say how that number is split and each insurer sets its own. We found no published rule for what happens when the limit underneath is below that minimum, so the safe move is to close the gap rather than find out during a claim.\nWhat to ask\nAsk your agent: \"What would it cost to raise my auto liability to 250/500?\" and \"Until I do, will my umbrella still respond the way I expect if I cause a serious accident?\" Then raise the limit to at least 250/500 and keep the answers in writing.\nSay this\nMy umbrella asks for 250/500 of liability on my auto policy and mine is 100/300, so I want it raised to match.\nSources\nInsurance Information Institute, Should I purchase an umbrella liability policy? (opens in a new tab) <https://www.iii.org/article/should-i-purchase-an-umbrella-liability-policy> , last verified 2026-10-10\nInsurance Information Institute, What is an umbrella liability policy? (opens in a new tab) <https://www.iii.org/article/what-is-an-umbrella-liability-policy> , last verified 2026-10-10\nNational Association of Insurance Commissioners, NAIC Consumer Shopping Tool for Auto Insurance (opens in a new tab) <https://content.naic.org/sites/default/files/inline-files/topic_transparency_readability_consumer_auto_tool.pdf> , last verified 2026-10-10\nRaise\nRaise your motorcycle liability limit to what your umbrella asks for\nWhy it matters\nA car policy does not cover a motorcycle, so a motorcycle needs its own liability policy, and that policy's limit is what pays first if you hurt someone while riding. An umbrella is extra liability coverage that starts paying only after the limits underneath are used up. You told us your umbrella asks for 500/500 on your motorcycle policy and yours has 250/500. We found no published standard for what an umbrella should require on a motorcycle, so your umbrella's own number is the one to go by, and it is worth confirming in writing.\nWhat to ask\nAsk your agent: \"What does my umbrella require on my motorcycle policy? Please put it in writing.\" and \"What would it cost to raise my motorcycle liability to 500/500?\" Then raise the limit to at least what your umbrella requires.\nSay this\nMy umbrella asks for 500/500 on my motorcycle policy and mine is 250/500, so I want it raised to match.\nSources\nNational Association of Insurance Commissioners, What Does Auto Insurance Cover? (opens in a new tab) <https://content.naic.org/article/what-you-should-know-about-auto-insurance-coverage> , last verified 2026-10-10\nInsurance Information Institute, Motorcycle insurance (opens in a new tab) <https://www.iii.org/article/motorcycle-insurance> , last verified 2026-10-10\nInsurance Information Institute, Should I purchase an umbrella liability policy? (opens in a new tab) <https://www.iii.org/article/should-i-purchase-an-umbrella-liability-policy> , last verified 2026-10-10\nRaise\nRaise your boat liability limit to what your umbrella asks for\nWhy it matters\nA home policy usually gives a boat little or no liability protection, so most of a boat's liability coverage comes from the boat's own policy. An umbrella is extra liability coverage that starts paying only after the limits underneath are used up, and the Insurance Information Institute notes that boat owners may want one. You told us your umbrella asks for 100/300 on your boat policy and yours has a limit lower than 100/300. We found no published standard for what an umbrella should require on a boat, so your umbrella's own number is the one to go by, and it is worth confirming in writing.\nWhat to ask\nAsk your agent: \"What does my umbrella require on my boat policy? Please put it in writing.\" and \"What would it cost to raise my boat liability to 100/300?\" Then raise the limit to at least what your umbrella requires.\nSay this\nMy umbrella asks for 100/300 on my boat policy and mine is lower than 100/300, so I want it raised to match.\nSources\nInsurance Information Institute, Boat insurance and safety (opens in a new tab) <https://www.iii.org/article/boat-insurance-and-safety> , last verified 2026-10-10\nNational Association of Insurance Commissioners, Summer Safety: Are You Insured for Summer Fun? (opens in a new tab) <https://content.naic.org/article/consumer-insight-summer-safety-are-you-insured-summer-fun> , last verified 2026-10-10\nInsurance Information Institute, Should I purchase an umbrella liability policy? (opens in a new tab) <https://www.iii.org/article/should-i-purchase-an-umbrella-liability-policy> , last verified 2026-10-10\nRaise\nAsk about adding gap coverage to your financed car\nWhy it matters\nIf your newest car is totaled or stolen, a regular collision or comprehensive claim pays what the car was worth, not what you still owe. Gap coverage pays the difference. It does not replace collision or comprehensive. Gap coverage matters most if you put less than 20 percent down, took a loan of 60 months or longer, or rolled money you still owed on an old car into this loan. A new car loses value quickly, and most lose about 20 percent of their value within a year. You told us you do not have gap coverage.\nWhat to ask\nAsk your auto insurer what gap coverage (also called loan/lease coverage) would cost on this car, and compare it with the price the dealer or lender gives you, since prices vary widely. If you roll it into the loan, you borrow more and pay interest on it. A dealer or lender generally cannot make you buy gap unless your sales contract explicitly says so. If you owe less than the car is worth, you may not need it.\nSay this\nIf my car is totaled, I want gap coverage to pay the difference between what it was worth and what I still owe.\nSources\nInsurance Information Institute, What is gap insurance? (opens in a new tab) <https://www.iii.org/article/what-is-gap-insurance> , last verified 2026-10-10\nConsumer Financial Protection Bureau, What is Guaranteed Asset Protection (GAP) insurance? (opens in a new tab) <https://www.consumerfinance.gov/ask-cfpb/what-is-guaranteed-asset-protection-gap-insurance-en-797/> , last verified 2026-10-10\nConsumer Financial Protection Bureau, Am I required to purchase an extended warranty, Guaranteed Asset Protection (GAP) insurance, or credit insurance from a lender or dealer to get an auto loan? (opens in a new tab) <https://www.consumerfinance.gov/ask-cfpb/am-i-required-to-purchase-an-extended-warranty-or-guaranteed-asset-protection-gap-insurance-from-a-lender-or-dealer-to-get-an-auto-loan-en-807/> , last verified 2026-10-10\nNational Association of Insurance Commissioners, A Shopping Tool for Auto Insurance (opens in a new tab) <https://content.naic.org/sites/default/files/consumer-auto-shopping-tool.pdf> , last verified 2026-10-10\nDrop (2)\nCoverage you may be paying for twice.\nDrop\nCheck whether you can skip the rental company's extra coverage\nWhy it matters\nThe extra coverage a rental company sells at the counter, called a collision or loss damage waiver, is not technically insurance. It relieves you of responsibility if the rental car is damaged or stolen. You may already have that protection. If you still carry collision and comprehensive on your own car (collision pays for crash damage, comprehensive for things like theft and hail), the coverage and deductible on your car generally apply to a rental you use for personal trips. Many credit cards cover rental car damage when you pay for the rental with the card, but you generally have to decline the rental company's waiver to use it. That coverage is usually secondary, meaning it pays only after your own car insurance or the rental company's coverage, and it may cover only your deductible. If nothing else covers the rental and you decline the waiver, you can owe for the damage, sometimes up to the car's full value. Check the current benefits guide for the card you carry to see whether it includes rental car coverage before paying separately. Card benefits change often and differ from card to card, so treat this as a prompt to check, not a promise of coverage. Your own card's current benefits guide has the final word.\nWhat to ask\nBefore your next rental, ask your car insurer whether your policy covers a rental car on personal trips, including theft and damage, and what your deductible would be. Rules vary, so get the answer from your insurer or your state's department of insurance. Then, if you plan to rely on a card, ask its issuer for the rental terms in writing: whether it pays first or second, and what you must do to qualify, which usually means paying with the card and declining the waiver. If both cover you, decline the waiver at the counter.\nSay this\nI want to confirm my own policy and my card already cover a rental car before I pay the rental company for the same protection.\nSources\nInsurance Information Institute, Rental car insurance (opens in a new tab) <https://www.iii.org/article/rental-car-insurance> , last verified 2026-10-10\nFederal Trade Commission, Renting a Car (opens in a new tab) <https://consumer.ftc.gov/articles/renting-car> , last verified 2026-10-10\nNational Association of Insurance Commissioners, Auto Insurance (opens in a new tab) <https://content.naic.org/consumer/auto-insurance.htm> , last verified 2026-10-10\nDrop\nCheck whether you are paying twice for roadside help\nWhy it matters\nTowing or roadside coverage on a car insurance policy pays for things like a tow when your car is disabled. The National Association of Insurance Commissioners (NAIC, a group of state insurance regulators) calls it an optional extra, separate from the basic policy, and says an auto club such as AAA or another membership may already include some of the same help. You told us you get roadside help from a credit card and roadside help that came with your car, so you may be paying for the same tow more than once. Plans differ, so ask each one what it covers. Check the current benefits guide for the card you carry to see whether it includes roadside help before paying separately. Card benefits change often and differ from card to card, so treat this as a prompt to check, not a promise of coverage. Your own card's current benefits guide has the final word.\nWhat to ask\nAsk each plan what it covers and what it costs, starting with your car insurer if you have a roadside add-on there. If another plan already gives you the same help, cancel the one you would use least.\nSay this\nI already get roadside help from another plan, so I want to know whether I need to keep paying for this one.\nSources\nNational Association of Insurance Commissioners, What Does Auto Insurance Cover? (opens in a new tab) <https://content.naic.org/article/what-you-should-know-about-auto-insurance-coverage> , last verified 2026-10-10\nNational Association of Insurance Commissioners, Does your vehicle have the right protection? Best practices for buying auto insurance (opens in a new tab) <https://content.naic.org/article/consumer-insight-does-your-vehicle-have-right-protection-best-practices-buying-auto-insurance> , last verified 2026-10-10\nNational Association of Insurance Commissioners, A Shopping Tool for Auto Insurance (opens in a new tab) <https://content.naic.org/sites/default/files/consumer-auto-shopping-tool.pdf> , last verified 2026-10-10\nQuestion (16)\nThings to ask your insurer or agent, because the right answer depends on your situation.\nQuestion\nAsk if your umbrella can add uninsured and underinsured motorist coverage\nWhy it matters\nUninsured and underinsured motorist coverage (UM/UIM) is an auto coverage that pays you and your passengers when the driver who hurt you has no insurance, drove off, or has too little to cover your loss. How much you have, and whether it is required, varies by state and by policy, and your state's department of insurance can tell you what applies to you. An umbrella is liability protection, so it responds when you are legally responsible for hurting someone else. UM/UIM is different, because it pays you. Whether your umbrella extends it depends on its own wording. If it does not, the UM/UIM limit on your auto policy is the most that coverage would pay you, and a serious injury could run past it.\nWhat to ask\nAsk your agent whether uninsured and underinsured motorist coverage can be added to your umbrella, and what it would cost. If it cannot, ask what the uninsured and underinsured limit on your auto policy is, so you know the most you could collect.\nSay this\nI want to know whether uninsured and underinsured motorist coverage can be added to my umbrella.\nSources\nNational Association of Insurance Commissioners, NAIC Consumer Shopping Tool for Auto Insurance (opens in a new tab) <https://content.naic.org/sites/default/files/inline-files/topic_transparency_readability_consumer_auto_tool.pdf> , last verified 2026-10-10\nInsurance Information Institute, Protect yourself against uninsured motorists (opens in a new tab) <https://www.iii.org/article/protect-yourself-against-uninsured-motorists> , last verified 2026-10-10\nNational Association of Insurance Commissioners, What Does Auto Insurance Cover? (opens in a new tab) <https://content.naic.org/article/what-you-should-know-about-auto-insurance-coverage> , last verified 2026-10-10\nNational Association of Insurance Commissioners, What’s an Umbrella Policy? (opens in a new tab) <https://content.naic.org/article/whats-umbrella-policy> , last verified 2026-10-10\nInsurance Information Institute, What is an umbrella liability policy? (opens in a new tab) <https://www.iii.org/article/what-is-an-umbrella-liability-policy> , last verified 2026-10-10\nQuestion\nAsk whether your umbrella covers your side business\nHigher priority\nWhy it matters\nYou said you have a side business or side gig. A personal umbrella is liability protection (it pays when you are legally responsible for hurting someone or damaging their property), and it typically leaves out claims that come from a business. It covers business-related liability only when the policies underneath it do, and your home policy usually does not cover liability from a business run at home. Whether a side gig counts as a business depends on how each policy defines it, so it is a question for your agent, not something to assume either way.\nWhat to ask\nTell your agent what your side business or side gig is and ask: does my umbrella cover claims that come from it? Ask too whether the policies underneath it, like your home and auto, cover it, because the umbrella follows what they cover. Then ask what else your umbrella leaves out.\nSay this\nI have a side business, and I want to know whether my umbrella covers claims that come from it.\nSources\nInsurance Information Institute, Business Vehicle Insurance (opens in a new tab) <https://www.iii.org/publication/insuring-your-business-small-business-owners-guide-to-insurance/specific-coverages/business-vehicle-insurance> , last verified 2026-10-10\nInsurance Information Institute, Insuring Your Home Business (opens in a new tab) <https://www.iii.org/article/insuring-your-home-business> , last verified 2026-10-10\nNational Association of Insurance Commissioners (Journal of Insurance Regulation), Social Media as a Factor in Personal Injury Underwriting: Risk, Rate and Regulation (opens in a new tab) <https://content.naic.org/sites/default/files/inline-files/JIR-ZA-39-01-EL_0.pdf> , last verified 2026-10-10\nNational Association of Insurance Commissioners, What’s an Umbrella Policy? (opens in a new tab) <https://content.naic.org/article/whats-umbrella-policy> , last verified 2026-10-10\nInsurance Information Institute, What is an umbrella liability policy? (opens in a new tab) <https://www.iii.org/article/what-is-an-umbrella-liability-policy> , last verified 2026-10-10\nQuestion\nAsk whether rental car coverage is worth adding\nWhy it matters\nYou told us your policy would not pay for a rental, so you would cover that cost yourself while your car is being repaired. Rental reimbursement pays a limited amount per day toward a rental car while your own car is being repaired after a covered claim. It is optional, and policies cap both the daily amount and the number of days. It is different from insurance for a car you rent on a trip. Like other optional coverages, it is priced separately and raises your premium, which is what you pay for the policy. In some states this coverage is tied to your collision and comprehensive coverage instead, so check your own policy.\nWhat to ask\nAsk your insurer what rental reimbursement would add to your premium and which daily and total limits you could choose. Whether it is worth it is your call: weigh the cost against what a rental would run for the days a repair could take.\nSay this\nI don't have rental reimbursement, so I want to know what it would cost and what it would pay.\nSources\nNational Association of Insurance Commissioners, A Shopping Tool for Auto Insurance (opens in a new tab) <https://content.naic.org/sites/default/files/consumer-auto-shopping-tool.pdf> , last verified 2026-10-10\nNational Association of Insurance Commissioners, What Does Auto Insurance Cover? (opens in a new tab) <https://content.naic.org/article/what-you-should-know-about-auto-insurance-coverage> , last verified 2026-10-10\nNational Association of Insurance Commissioners, Does your vehicle have the right protection? Best practices for buying auto insurance (opens in a new tab) <https://content.naic.org/article/consumer-insight-does-your-vehicle-have-right-protection-best-practices-buying-auto-insurance> , last verified 2026-10-10\nNational Association of Insurance Commissioners, A Consumer's Guide to Auto Insurance (opens in a new tab) <https://content.naic.org/sites/default/files/publication-aut-pp-consumer-auto.pdf> , last verified 2026-10-10\nQuestion\nAsk about adding sewer and drain backup coverage\nHigher priority\nWhy it matters\nSewer and drain backup is when sewage or water comes back up through your drains or sewer line and damages your floors, walls and belongings. A typical home policy does not cover it, and you told us yours does not. A flood policy would not fill the gap: the federal flood program pays for sewer backup only when the backup is a direct result of a flood. The usual fix is an add-on to your home policy, called an endorsement or rider, which most insurers offer for a small extra cost.\nWhat to ask\nAsk your insurer or agent for a quote to add sewer and drain backup coverage to your home policy, and ask three things: what it would cost, the most it would pay (the limit), and whether it covers sump pump overflow, which many policies leave out, if you have a sump pump (the pump that keeps a basement dry).\nSay this\nMy home policy does not cover sewer or drain backup, so I want a quote to add it and a limit I can count on.\nSources\nNational Association of Insurance Commissioners, Homeowners Insurance (opens in a new tab) <https://content.naic.org/insurance-topics/homeowners-insurance> , last verified 2026-10-10\nInsurance Information Institute, Which disasters are covered by homeowners insurance? (opens in a new tab) <https://www.iii.org/article/which-disasters-are-covered-by-homeowners-insurance> , last verified 2026-10-10\nInsurance Information Institute, How to protect your home from water damage (opens in a new tab) <https://www.iii.org/article/how-to-protect-your-home-from-water-damage> , last verified 2026-10-10\nInsurance Information Institute, Protect your house from sewer backups (opens in a new tab) <https://www.iii.org/article/protect-your-house-from-sewer-backups> , last verified 2026-10-10\nNational Association of Insurance Commissioners, Searching for a Homeowners Insurance Policy? Tips to Get the Most Value (opens in a new tab) <https://content.naic.org/article/consumer_insight_searching_homeowners_insurance_policy_tips_get_most_value.htm> , last verified 2026-10-10\nNational Association of Insurance Commissioners, What is an Insurance Endorsement or Rider? (opens in a new tab) <https://content.naic.org/article/consumer-insight-what-insurance-endorsement-or-rider> , last verified 2026-10-10\nFEMA National Flood Insurance Program (FloodSmart), Floodsmart | The National Flood Insurance Program (opens in a new tab) <https://www.floodsmart.gov/> , last verified 2026-10-10\nQuestion\nConfirm your child's renters policy covers what they own\nWhy it matters\nA student who lives off campus is likely not covered by your home policy, and some insurers may not extend student coverage to an apartment rented in the student's name. So the renters policy you pay for is probably not a duplicate. The building owner's insurance and the school's insurance do not cover your child's personal items either, so without their own policy your child would probably have nothing to fall back on.\nWhat to ask\nAsk your home insurer whether your policy reaches a place your child rents off campus; it likely does not. If it does not, your child probably needs their own renters policy, so ask the renters insurer whether its limit is enough to replace what your child owns and what the deductible is.\nSay this\nMy child rents off campus, so I want to confirm my home policy does not reach them and that their renters policy limit is enough.\nSources\nInsurance Information Institute, Protecting your college student from on-campus losses (opens in a new tab) <https://www.iii.org/article/protecting-your-college-student-from-on-campus-losses> , last verified 2026-10-10\nNational Association of Insurance Commissioners, Do I Need Renters Insurance For College? (opens in a new tab) <https://content.naic.org/article/do-i-need-renters-insurance-college> , last verified 2026-10-10\nNational Association of Insurance Commissioners, Renters Insurance for College Students (opens in a new tab) <https://content.naic.org/article/consumer-insight-renters-insurance-college-students> , last verified 2026-10-10\nInsurance Information Institute, Am I Covered? (opens in a new tab) <https://www.iii.org/article/am-i-covered> , last verified 2026-10-10\nInsurance Information Institute, Renters Insurance (opens in a new tab) <https://www.iii.org/article/renters-insurance> , last verified 2026-10-10\nQuestion\nAsk how your auto medical coverage works with your health plan\nWhy it matters\nYour auto policy has coverage that pays medical bills for you and your passengers after a crash. Depending on your policy it may be called medical payments (MedPay) or personal injury protection (PIP). In states with no-fault rules, your own auto insurer pays for these injuries no matter who caused the crash. Your health plan can pay for crash injuries too, so the two can overlap. How they work together depends on your state's rules and the exact wording of both policies, so ask both insurers. Some states require this coverage, and if yours does you may not be able to drop it. Homeowners policies have a similar-sounding add-on, medical payments to others, but it pays for guests hurt at your home, not for your own household, so it is not part of this overlap.\nWhat to ask\nAsk your auto insurer what your medical payments or PIP coverage pays for, whether your state requires you to carry it, and how it works alongside your health plan after a crash, including whether your policy has an option to coordinate the two. Your state's department of insurance can also tell you what your state requires. Then ask your health insurer the same thing from their side. If your state does not require it, ask what your premium would be without it and decide whether you still want it.\nSay this\nI have both health insurance and auto medical coverage, so I want to know how the two work together and whether I still need both.\nSources\nNational Association of Insurance Commissioners, What Does Auto Insurance Cover? (opens in a new tab) <https://content.naic.org/article/what-you-should-know-about-auto-insurance-coverage> , last verified 2026-10-10\nInsurance Information Institute, Auto insurance basics—understanding your coverage (opens in a new tab) <https://www.iii.org/article/auto-insurance-basics-understanding-your-coverage> , last verified 2026-10-10\nNational Association of Insurance Commissioners, Coordination of Benefits Model Regulation (opens in a new tab) <https://content.naic.org/sites/default/files/model-law-120.pdf> , last verified 2026-10-10\nInsurance Information Institute, What is covered by standard homeowners insurance? (opens in a new tab) <https://www.iii.org/article/what-is-covered-by-standard-homeowners-insurance> , last verified 2026-10-10\nQuestion\nAsk whether your extra health policies are worth their cost\nWhy it matters\nYou told us you pay for critical illness and cancer insurance on top of your main health plan. Hospital indemnity and other fixed indemnity policies pay you a set amount of cash, and federal regulators say that kind of coverage is not a substitute for comprehensive health coverage. Accident, cancer and critical illness policies are different products. They may pay you a set amount of cash when a covered event happens or they may work another way, so check how yours pays. Federal law generally treats these policies as add-ons that fall outside most of the rules for major medical plans, so they sit alongside your main plan, not in place of it. Some comprehensive-sounding health plan ads are really limited-benefit or discount plans, so it helps to know exactly what you bought. If a big bill would be hard to cover from savings, the payout may be a fair reason to keep it.\nWhat to ask\nAsk your benefits office or the company that sells it which events trigger a payout, whether it pays a set amount or repays specific costs, how much it pays, and what you pay in premiums over a year. Compare that with what your main plan and your savings already handle, and keep it only if the payout is worth the cost to you. If open enrollment is coming up at your job or on the Marketplace, this is a good moment to ask. Your employer sets its own open enrollment dates, so check with them. On HealthCare.gov, open enrollment for 2027 coverage runs November 1 to January 15.\nSay this\nI pay for critical illness and cancer insurance on top of my health plan, and I want to confirm what it pays and whether that is worth the premium.\nSources\nU.S. Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services, Short-Term, Limited-Duration Insurance and Independent, Noncoordinated Excepted Benefits Coverage (CMS-9904-F) Fact Sheet (opens in a new tab) <https://www.cms.gov/newsroom/fact-sheets/short-term-limited-duration-insurance-and-independent-noncoordinated-excepted-benefits-coverage-cms> , last verified 2026-10-10\nU.S. Department of Labor (Employee Benefits Security Administration), with HHS and Treasury, FAQs about Affordable Care Act Implementation Part 72 (opens in a new tab) <https://www.dol.gov/agencies/ebsa/about-ebsa/our-activities/resource-center/faqs/aca-part-72> , last verified 2026-10-10\nU.S. Federal Trade Commission (Consumer Advice), How to avoid health insurance scams this open enrollment season (opens in a new tab) <https://consumer.ftc.gov/consumer-alerts/2025/12/how-avoid-health-insurance-scams-open-enrollment-season> , last verified 2026-10-10\nHealthCare.gov (U.S. Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services), When can you get health insurance? | HealthCare.gov (opens in a new tab) <https://www.healthcare.gov/quick-guide/dates-and-deadlines/> , last verified 2026-10-10\nHealthCare.gov (U.S. Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services), Open Enrollment Period - Glossary | HealthCare.gov (opens in a new tab) <https://www.healthcare.gov/glossary/open-enrollment-period/> , last verified 2026-10-10\nQuestion\nAsk whether your high-deductible plan can have a health savings account (HSA)\nWhy it matters\nYou told us your plan is a high-deductible plan but you do not have a health savings account (HSA). An HSA is a savings account for medical costs that belongs to you, and you can put money in it only with a qualifying high-deductible plan. Under federal tax rules, money you put in is tax-deductible, it grows without tax, and withdrawals for qualified medical costs are not taxed. Unused money carries over from year to year and goes with you if you change jobs. For 2026 the IRS minimum deductible for a qualifying plan is $1,700 for one person and $3,400 for a family; for 2027 it is $1,750 and $3,500. Meeting that minimum alone does not make a plan HSA-eligible, so the plan itself has to be checked.\nWhat to ask\nAsk your plan administrator or benefits office whether your plan is HSA-eligible and, if it is, how to open an HSA and set up contributions. If open enrollment is coming up at your job or on the Marketplace, this is a good moment to ask. Your employer sets its own open enrollment dates, so check with them. On HealthCare.gov, open enrollment for 2027 coverage runs November 1 to January 15.\nSay this\nI have a high-deductible plan and no HSA, so I want to know whether my plan qualifies for one.\nSources\nInternal Revenue Service, Publication 969 (2025), Health Savings Accounts and Other Tax-Favored Health Plans (opens in a new tab) <https://www.irs.gov/publications/p969> , last verified 2026-10-10\nHealthCare.gov (U.S. Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services), Health Savings Account (HSA) - Glossary | HealthCare.gov (opens in a new tab) <https://www.healthcare.gov/glossary/health-savings-account-hsa/> , last verified 2026-10-10\nInternal Revenue Service, Rev. Proc. 2026-24 (opens in a new tab) <https://www.irs.gov/pub/irs-drop/rp-26-24.pdf> , last verified 2026-10-10\nInternal Revenue Service, Rev. Proc. 2025-19 (opens in a new tab) <https://www.irs.gov/pub/irs-drop/rp-25-19.pdf> , last verified 2026-10-10\nHealthCare.gov (U.S. Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services), When can you get health insurance? | HealthCare.gov (opens in a new tab) <https://www.healthcare.gov/quick-guide/dates-and-deadlines/> , last verified 2026-10-10\nHealthCare.gov (U.S. Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services), Open Enrollment Period - Glossary | HealthCare.gov (opens in a new tab) <https://www.healthcare.gov/glossary/open-enrollment-period/> , last verified 2026-10-10\nQuestion\nWeigh your auto deductible against the cash you could reach\nHigher priority\nWhy it matters\nA deductible is the part of a claim you pay before your insurer pays, and a bigger deductible generally means a lower premium. That trade only works if you can cover the deductible on the day you need it. You told us your collision deductible is $5,000 or more and that you could cover under $1,000 from savings, so a claim could cost you more up front than you have set aside. This is not a verdict on your policy. It is a gap between the bill a claim could hand you and the cash you have, and the choice is yours: build up your cash cushion, or reconsider the deductible. For context, in the Federal Reserve's 2025 survey, 30% of adults had a major unexpected vehicle repair or replacement bill in the past year, and about 39% of adults said the most they could cover from savings alone was under $1,000.\nWhat to ask\nYou can build up cash toward your deductible, or ask your agent what your premium would be at a lower collision deductible and which options your policy offers. Then choose a deductible you could pay tomorrow.\nSay this\nI could not cover my collision deductible from savings today, so I want to see what a lower deductible would cost.\nSources\nInsurance Information Institute, Understanding your insurance deductibles (opens in a new tab) <https://www.iii.org/article/understanding-your-insurance-deductibles> , last verified 2026-10-10\nNational Association of Insurance Commissioners, Tips for Saving on your Auto Insurance (opens in a new tab) <https://content.naic.org/article/consumer-insight-tips-saving-your-auto-insurance> , last verified 2026-10-10\nBoard of Governors of the Federal Reserve System, Report on the Economic Well-Being of U.S. Households in 2025 - May 2026: Savings and Investments (opens in a new tab) <https://www.federalreserve.gov/publications/2026-economic-well-being-of-us-households-in-2025-savings-investments.htm> , last verified 2026-10-10\nBoard of Governors of the Federal Reserve System, Report on the Economic Well-Being of U.S. Households in 2025 - May 2026: Economic Hardships (opens in a new tab) <https://www.federalreserve.gov/publications/2026-economic-well-being-of-us-households-in-2025-economic-hardships.htm> , last verified 2026-10-10\nQuestion\nWeigh your home deductible against the cash you could reach\nHigher priority\nWhy it matters\nA deductible is the part of a claim you pay before your insurer pays, and a bigger deductible generally means a lower premium. That trade only works if you can cover the deductible on the day you need it. You told us your home policy's deductible is $1,000 to $4,999 and that you could cover under $1,000 from savings, so a claim could cost you more up front than you have set aside. This is not a verdict on your policy. It is a gap between the bill a claim could hand you and the cash you have, and the choice is yours: build up your cash cushion, or reconsider the deductible. Wind, hail and earthquake claims often carry a percentage deductible instead of a dollar one (typically 1% to 5% for wind or hail, and 2% to 20% of the home's replacement value for earthquake, depending on location), so those claims can cost far more than the standard deductible you gave us. For context, in the Federal Reserve's 2025 survey, 22% of adults had a major unexpected house or appliance repair bill in the past year, and about 39% of adults said the most they could cover from savings alone was under $1,000.\nWhat to ask\nYou can build up cash toward your deductible, or ask your agent what your premium would be at a lower deductible. Also ask whether you have a percentage deductible for wind, hail or earthquake, and what that works out to in dollars for your home. Then choose a deductible you could pay tomorrow.\nSay this\nI could not cover my home deductible from savings today, so I want to see what a lower deductible would cost.\nSources\nInsurance Information Institute, Understanding your insurance deductibles (opens in a new tab) <https://www.iii.org/article/understanding-your-insurance-deductibles> , last verified 2026-10-10\nBoard of Governors of the Federal Reserve System, Report on the Economic Well-Being of U.S. Households in 2025 - May 2026: Savings and Investments (opens in a new tab) <https://www.federalreserve.gov/publications/2026-economic-well-being-of-us-households-in-2025-savings-investments.htm> , last verified 2026-10-10\nBoard of Governors of the Federal Reserve System, Report on the Economic Well-Being of U.S. Households in 2025 - May 2026: Economic Hardships (opens in a new tab) <https://www.federalreserve.gov/publications/2026-economic-well-being-of-us-households-in-2025-economic-hardships.htm> , last verified 2026-10-10\nQuestion\nWeigh your health deductible against the cash you could reach\nHigher priority\nWhy it matters\nA deductible is what you pay for covered health care before your plan starts to pay. You told us your health plan's deductible is $1,000 to $4,999 and that you could cover under $1,000 from savings, so a medical bill could cost you more up front than you have set aside. Your plan's out-of-pocket maximum (the most you pay for covered care in a plan year) can be higher than the deductible, so a bad year could cost more, and we are not asking about that. This is not a verdict on your plan. It is a gap between a medical bill and the cash you have, and the choice is yours: build up your cash cushion, or reconsider the deductible. For context, in the Federal Reserve's 2025 survey, 21% of adults had an unexpected major medical expense in the past year, and about 39% of adults said the most they could cover from savings alone was under $1,000.\nWhat to ask\nAsk your insurer or benefits office what your deductible and out-of-pocket maximum are for the coming year, and what plan options with a lower deductible would cost. You can also build up cash toward the deductible. If open enrollment is coming up at your job or on the Marketplace, this is a good moment to ask. Your employer sets its own open enrollment dates, so check with them. On HealthCare.gov, open enrollment for 2027 coverage runs November 1 to January 15.\nSay this\nI could not cover my health plan deductible from savings today, so I want to compare options with a lower deductible.\nSources\nHealthCare.gov (U.S. Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services), Deductible - Glossary | HealthCare.gov (opens in a new tab) <https://www.healthcare.gov/glossary/deductible/> , last verified 2026-10-10\nHealthCare.gov (U.S. Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services), Out-of-pocket maximum/limit - Glossary | HealthCare.gov (opens in a new tab) <https://www.healthcare.gov/glossary/out-of-pocket-maximum-limit/> , last verified 2026-10-10\nBoard of Governors of the Federal Reserve System, Report on the Economic Well-Being of U.S. Households in 2025 - May 2026: Savings and Investments (opens in a new tab) <https://www.federalreserve.gov/publications/2026-economic-well-being-of-us-households-in-2025-savings-investments.htm> , last verified 2026-10-10\nBoard of Governors of the Federal Reserve System, Report on the Economic Well-Being of U.S. Households in 2025 - May 2026: Economic Hardships (opens in a new tab) <https://www.federalreserve.gov/publications/2026-economic-well-being-of-us-households-in-2025-economic-hardships.htm> , last verified 2026-10-10\nHealthCare.gov (U.S. Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services), When can you get health insurance? | HealthCare.gov (opens in a new tab) <https://www.healthcare.gov/quick-guide/dates-and-deadlines/> , last verified 2026-10-10\nHealthCare.gov (U.S. Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services), Open Enrollment Period - Glossary | HealthCare.gov (opens in a new tab) <https://www.healthcare.gov/glossary/open-enrollment-period/> , last verified 2026-10-10\nQuestion\nCheck where the towing help on your motorcycle or boat comes from\nWhy it matters\nThe National Association of Insurance Commissioners (NAIC, a group of state insurance regulators) says a car insurance policy does not cover motorcycles, so the towing help on your car policy generally does not reach one. The Insurance Information Institute (Triple-I, an insurance industry research group) lists towing after an accident among the common and optional coverages on a boat policy, so check whether yours already has it. So tow help for your motorcycle or boat may come from its own policy, a club or a card. We did not find anything saying these plans usually repeat each other, so this is a question to ask, not a verdict. Card benefits change often and differ from card to card, so treat this as a prompt to check, not a promise of coverage. Your own card's current benefits guide has the final word.\nWhat to ask\nAsk the company that insures your motorcycle or boat exactly what its towing coverage includes, for example whether it covers a breakdown or only a tow after an accident. Also ask the credit card whether the help reaches your motorcycle or boat at all. If two of them cover the same thing, keep the one you would actually call.\nSay this\nI want to know whether the towing plan on my motorcycle or boat repeats help I already get from another plan.\nSources\nNational Association of Insurance Commissioners, What Does Auto Insurance Cover? (opens in a new tab) <https://content.naic.org/article/what-you-should-know-about-auto-insurance-coverage> , last verified 2026-10-10\nInsurance Information Institute, Boat insurance and safety (opens in a new tab) <https://www.iii.org/article/boat-insurance-and-safety> , last verified 2026-10-10\nQuestion\nCheck what you already have before paying for travel insurance\nWhy it matters\nTravel insurance can bundle several kinds of coverage: trip cancellation, interruption and delay, emergency medical and evacuation, accidental death, and lost or delayed baggage. The National Association of Insurance Commissioners (NAIC, a group of state insurance regulators) says a policy usually costs about 5% to 10% of the trip price, and each policy sets its own limits and covered reasons. Some of it may repeat what you already have. Some credit cards include trip cancellation and lost baggage coverage, a home or renters policy usually covers belongings lost or stolen while you travel, and the accidental death coverage in a travel policy may duplicate your life insurance. Medical coverage is the part to be careful with, because your health plan may not cover care abroad. Check the current benefits guide for the card you carry to see whether it includes trip protection before paying separately. Card benefits change often and differ from card to card, so treat this as a prompt to check, not a promise of coverage. Your own card's current benefits guide has the final word.\nWhat to ask\nBefore you buy, ask the issuer of the card you would book with for its trip protection terms, including the reasons it covers and its limits. Check your home or renters policy for lost or stolen baggage, and your life insurance if the travel policy includes accidental death. Then buy only the parts you do not already have. Ask your health insurer whether it covers you abroad before you give up medical coverage.\nSay this\nI want to compare this policy with any trip protection on my card and the coverage I already have, so I only pay for what is missing.\nSources\nNational Association of Insurance Commissioners, Should I Get Travel Insurance? (opens in a new tab) <https://content.naic.org/article/should-i-get-travel-insurance> , last verified 2026-10-10\nInsurance Information Institute, Should you buy travel insurance? (opens in a new tab) <https://www.iii.org/article/should-you-buy-travel-insurance> , last verified 2026-10-10\nQuestion\nCheck whether a card or your home or renters policy already covers your phone\nWhy it matters\nYou told us you pay a monthly fee to insure your phone. Before you keep paying it, check two places that may already cover the same phone. Your homeowners or renters policy generally covers your belongings, including things you carry away from home, up to a limit, and some insurers cap the away-from-home part. Ask whether it covers a phone and what you would pay out of pocket first. Check the current benefits guide for the card you carry to see whether it includes cell phone protection before paying separately. Card benefits change often and differ from card to card, so treat this as a prompt to check, not a promise of coverage. Your own card's current benefits guide has the final word.\nWhat to ask\nAsk the issuer of any card you carry whether it protects your phone and what it requires. Ask your home or renters insurer whether a phone is covered and what your deductible would be. Keep the carrier plan until one of them confirms coverage.\nSay this\nI may already have coverage for my phone through a card or my home or renters policy, so I want to confirm that before I keep paying for a separate plan.\nSources\nInsurance Information Institute, What is covered by standard homeowners insurance? (opens in a new tab) <https://www.iii.org/article/what-is-covered-by-standard-homeowners-insurance> , last verified 2026-10-10\nInsurance Information Institute, Renters Insurance (opens in a new tab) <https://www.iii.org/article/renters-insurance> , last verified 2026-10-10\nQuestion\nCheck before you pay extra for another extended warranty\nWhy it matters\nAn extended warranty or service contract costs extra and is sold separately from the product. It is different from the warranty that may come with the product, and the Federal Trade Commission (FTC, a federal consumer protection agency) warns that the product's own warranty may already cover the same repairs for the same period. The FTC says one may not be worth the cost if the product is unlikely to need repairs or if it adds no coverage beyond the warranty that came with the product. It also says to look for hidden costs such as deductibles, per-repair fees and shipping, and that setting the money aside in savings for repairs may be a better option. Check the current benefits guide for the card you carry to see whether it includes an extended warranty benefit before paying separately. Card benefits change often and differ from card to card, so treat this as a prompt to check, not a promise of coverage. Your own card's current benefits guide has the final word.\nWhat to ask\nBefore you say yes to a plan, ask the seller what it covers beyond the product's own warranty, what the deductibles, per-repair fees and shipping costs are, and who stands behind the contract. Check whether the card you would pay with extends the manufacturer's warranty. If the plan adds nothing you do not already have, say no. If you already pay for one, ask the company what it covers beyond the manufacturer's warranty and whether you can cancel.\nSay this\nI want to know what this plan covers that the product's own warranty and my card do not before I pay for it.\nSources\nFederal Trade Commission, Extended Warranties and Service Contracts (opens in a new tab) <https://consumer.ftc.gov/articles/extended-warranties-and-service-contracts> , last verified 2026-10-10\nFederal Trade Commission, Warranties (opens in a new tab) <https://consumer.ftc.gov/articles/warranties> , last verified 2026-10-10\nQuestion\nAsk how your accidental death coverage fits with your life insurance\nWhy it matters\nAccidental death coverage, often called AD&D (accidental death and dismemberment), pays a stated amount only when an accident, or certain kinds of accident, causes a death or a loss. Regular life insurance pays your beneficiaries if you die while the policy is active; a term policy, for example, pays if you die during its term. Read your policy for how it defines an accident and for its exclusions, and check what it counts as a loss. You told us you do not have regular life insurance. Accidental death coverage is not a substitute for it: it pays only when an accident causes a death or a loss, while regular life insurance pays if you die during its term. If your travel insurance includes accidental death coverage, the Insurance Information Institute (Triple-I, an insurance industry research group) says that can duplicate your life insurance too.\nWhat to ask\nAsk your agent or benefits office what regular life insurance would cost and whether you need it, and check what your accidental death coverage actually pays, for which kinds of accident and for which losses.\nSay this\nI have accidental death coverage but no regular life insurance, so I want to know whether that covers what my family would need.\nSources\nNational Association of Insurance Commissioners, Glossary of Insurance Terms (opens in a new tab) <https://content.naic.org/glossary-insurance-terms> , last verified 2026-10-10\nNational Association of Insurance Commissioners, Life Insurance (opens in a new tab) <https://content.naic.org/consumer/life-insurance.htm> , last verified 2026-10-10\nNational Association of Insurance Commissioners, Life Insurance Roadmap (opens in a new tab) <https://content.naic.org/article/consumer-insight-life-insurance-roadmap> , last verified 2026-10-10\nInsurance Information Institute, Should you buy travel insurance? (opens in a new tab) <https://www.iii.org/article/should-you-buy-travel-insurance> , last verified 2026-10-10\nYour answers stay on this page and are not saved. Nothing about them is counted or sent anywhere. Close or refresh the page and they are gone.\n[Back] [Start over]\n== known ==\nAuto insurance\nHomeowners insurance\nRenters insurance\nUmbrella insurance\nHealth insurance\nMotorcycle insurance\nBoat insurance\nRental car insurance\nA towing or roadside plan\nTravel insurance for a trip\nPhone insurance\nExtended warranties on electronics or appliances\nAccidental death coverage\n== progress ==\n100% Your list"}
